output_text:
name: dotnet-channels
Implements high-performance, thread-safe producer/consumer queues with `System.Threading.Channels`: `Channel<T>`, bounded/unbounded channels, backpressure, capacity, `IAsyncEnumerable`, graceful shutdown/draining, and hosted services. See [skill:dotnet-background-services] and [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns].

A `Channel<T>` has separate thread-safe `ChannelWriter<T>` and `ChannelReader<T>` endpoints.

Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity) provides back-pressure and capped memory; Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>() grows without limit and can exhaust memory under load. Bounded channels are preferred for production; unbounded channels are for guaranteed-low-volume/prototyping or when you control the producer rate.

var bounded = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(new BoundedChannelOptions(capacity: 1000)
FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait

var unbounded = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();

`FullMode` controls full-channel behavior: `Wait` blocks `WriteAsync`; `DropOldest` drops the oldest; `DropNewest` drops the item being written; `DropWrite` drops the item being written and returns `false` from `TryWrite`.

var telemetryChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<SensorReading>(new BoundedChannelOptions(500)
FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest

var logChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<LogEntry>(new BoundedChannelOptions(10_000)
FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropWrite
if (!logChannel.Writer.TryWrite(entry))

Starting in .NET 7, bounded channels with drop modes accept an `itemDropped` callback that fires whenever an item is discarded. Use this for metrics, logging, or resource cleanup on dropped items.

var channel = Channel.CreateBounded(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest
itemDropped: (item, writer) =>
logger.LogWarning("Dropped item due to channel overflow: {Id}", item.Id);
(item as IDisposable)?.Dispose();

The callback receives the dropped item and the `ChannelWriter<T>`.

await writer.WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken);
if (!writer.TryWrite(item))

Multiple producers can call `WriteAsync` or `TryWrite` concurrently without external locking. Call `Complete()` or `TryComplete()` when no more items will be produced. `TryComplete` is idempotent—safe to call multiple times.

writer.TryComplete(new InvalidOperationException("Source failed"));

Preferred consumer pattern:
while (await reader.WaitToReadAsync(cancellationToken))
while (reader.TryRead(out var item))
await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);

This drains available items before awaiting again. Simpler:
var item = await reader.ReadAsync(cancellationToken);
await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);

Multiple consumer tasks scale processing; each item is read by exactly one consumer. `ReadAllAsync()` returns an `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`:
await foreach (var item in reader.ReadAllAsync(cancellationToken))
await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);

The loop exits when writer calls `Complete()` and all items are consumed. ASP.NET Core streaming:
app.MapGet("/api/events/stream", (
ChannelReader<ServerEvent> reader,
CancellationToken ct) => reader.ReadAllAsync(ct));

With the `System.Linq.Async` NuGet package:
await foreach (var batch in reader.ReadAllAsync(ct)
.Where(item => item.Priority >= Priority.High)
.Buffer(50)
await BulkProcessAsync(batch, ct);

Producer errors:
channel.Writer.TryComplete(ex);

`ReadAllAsync` propagates the error. Preserve cancellation-token propagation. `SingleReader = true` or `SingleWriter = true` enables lock-free optimizations; the channel trusts these hints—violating them (multiple concurrent readers when `SingleReader = true`) causes data corruption.

See [references/detailed-examples.md](references/detailed-examples.md) for complete code samples and advanced patterns.
